\paragraph{Findings.}
Reading the columns of Table~\ref{tab:lambda_ablation} from left to
right, the MSR on CICIoT2023 varies from ${{LAM_CIC_10_MSR}}$ at
$\lambda=0.10$ to ${{LAM_CIC_50_MSR}}$ at $\lambda=0.50$, a span of
${{CIC_MSR_SPAN_PP}}$ percentage points. The default $\lambda=0.25$
achieves ${{LAM_CIC_25_MSR}}$ with the lowest standard deviation
(${{LAM_CIC_25_MSR_STD}}$), which places it on the plateau of the
sensitivity curve rather than at a sharp peak. One detail is worth
flagging: $\lambda=0.50$ reaches a marginally higher MSR
(${{LAM_CIC_50_MSR}}$) but a lower mean reward
(${{LAM_CIC_50_REWARD}}$ vs.\ ${{LAM_CIC_25_REWARD}}$), so a larger
shaping weight trades a small amount of raw reward for classification
accuracy. The Jain fairness index remains $\ge {{LAM_CIC_10_JFI}}$
across the entire tested range.

Taken together, these numbers make the robustness claim in the
Abstract concrete: the framework is insensitive to the choice of
$\lambda$ in $[0.10, 0.50]$, and the ${{CIC_MSR_SPAN_PP}}$-pp span
is too small to change any qualitative conclusion drawn in the rest
of the manuscript. With the CICIoT2023 picture stable, we now turn
to the backbone-selection layer of Strategy~B.
